prompt:
Implement a PBR material editor using Three.js. Adjust the material parameters of DamagedHelmet in real time.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/ScatteringSkull.glb;
(2) Center the model;

[Material Parameter Sliders]
(3) Roughness slider (id="roughness", range 0-1, step 0.01);
(4) Metalness slider (id="metalness", range 0-1, step 0.01);
(5) Normal Scale slider (id="normalScale", range 0-2, step 0.1);
(6) Emissive Intensity slider (id="emissive", range 0-3, step 0.1);
(7) Base color picker (id="baseColor", type="color");
(8) All sliders must affect the model material in real time;

[Environment Map]
(9) 3 environment preset buttons: Studio, Outdoor, Night;
(10) When switching, use PMREMGenerator to generate an envMap and apply it;

[OrbitControls]
(11) Allow rotational viewing;

[HUD Display]
(12) HUD in the upper-left corner:
  - Current environment name (id="envName")
  - Roughness value (id="roughnessVal")
  - Metalness value (id="metalnessVal")

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
  - modelLoaded: boolean
  - roughness: number
  - metalness: number
  - normalScale: number
  - emissiveIntensity: number
  - envMap: string (current environment name)

Output a standalone HTML file.
